@* How to play: the game's ideas, aims and scoring, for a player rather than an engine (that is rules.md).
   Shown over the lobby or the table, from the page's settings, until closed. *@

<div class="guide-backdrop">
    <section class="guide" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="guide-title">
        <header class="guide-head">
            <h2 id="guide-title">How to play</h2>
            <button class="close" @onclick="this.OnClose">Close</button>
        </header>

        <h3>The aim</h3>
        <p>
            Four armies play, in two teams: allies sit opposite each other. Your team wins by scoring more points
            than the other: by taking pieces, and by a few bonuses below. There is no checkmate. Usually the game
            ends when everyone agrees it should, and then points decide.
        </p>

        <h3>Your army</h3>
        <p>
            Each army has a Raja (the king), an Elephant, a Horse, a Boat and four Pawns. The square your Raja
            starts on is your <strong>throne</strong>. Each army's Pawns move toward the middle of the board, so
            "forward" is different for each seat.
        </p>
        <table class="guide-pieces">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Piece</th><th>Moves</th><th>Taking it scores</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Raja</td><td>One square in any direction.</td><td>5</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Elephant</td><td>Any distance straight along a row or column, like a chess rook.</td><td>4</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Horse</td><td>Like a chess knight, jumping.</td><td>3</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Boat</td><td>Exactly two squares diagonally, jumping over the square between.</td><td>2</td></tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Pawn</td>
                    <td>
                        One square forward; it takes one square diagonally forward. Two allied Pawns back to back,
                        each facing the other, may swap places, as a Pawn's move.
                    </td>
                    <td>1</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Every piece but the Pawn takes the way it moves, by landing on an enemy.</p>

        <h3>Your turn</h3>
        <p>On your turn, you do one of these:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Move</strong> any one of your pieces, without rolling.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>Roll</strong> two dice. Each die lets you move one piece of the kind it shows: 1 a Boat,
                2 a Horse, 3 or 4 an Elephant, 5 or 6 a Pawn or the Raja. Use both, one or neither, in either
                order. A die nothing can use is lost.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Pass</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Nothing is ever compulsory. The very first turn of the game is the roll-off's winner playing the die
            that won it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Doubles</strong> let you make two moves with the kind they show (with one piece or two), or,
            instead, bring a captured piece of that kind back home: it <strong>escapes</strong>, taking any enemy
            on its square, and your turn ends.
        </p>

        <h3>Points</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Taking a piece scores its points, which you keep even if the piece comes back later.</li>
            <li><strong>+1</strong> each time your Raja enters an enemy's throne.</li>
            <li><strong>+2</strong> for taking an enemy Raja on its own throne.</li>
            <li><strong>+1</strong> for each prisoner going home, either way, in a ransom you accept.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>The empire:</strong> when the last enemy Raja is taken, each player of the team that took it
                who commands an ally's army (see below) scores <strong>+1</strong>. If the one who took it commands
                an ally's army, they score <strong>+2</strong> more, or <strong>+4</strong> if they took it on its
                throne. It can happen again, if an enemy Raja comes back and is taken again.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Thrones and command</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                While your Raja sits on another seat's throne, ally's or enemy's, you may move that army as well as
                your own on your turns. While your ally has no Raja on the board, you may move their army too.
                Either way, you command that army.
            </li>
            <li>
                While your ally's Raja sits on your throne, or you have no Raja, your ally may take your turn for
                you, if you hand it over ("Let ... take over").
            </li>
            <li>
                While an enemy Raja sits on your throne, you must roll. You make the first move; the enemy makes the
                second, with your army, unless your first move took that Raja.
            </li>
            <li>An enemy army you command may take any piece except your own team's.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Prisoners</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                The pieces you take are your prisoners. A Pawn reaching the far edge may become any captured piece,
                now or on a later turn.
            </li>
            <li>
                On your turn you may offer another player a <strong>ransom</strong>: a trade of prisoners, any
                number for any number. It happens only if they accept. Pieces given back return as near their
                starting squares as they can, never taking anything.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The end</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Anyone may press <strong>End game</strong> to ask the others to end it. If everyone agrees, points
                decide; if anyone refuses, play goes on.
            </li>
            <li>A team wins outright when the other team has no pieces left. If only Rajas are left, it is a draw.</li>
            <li>A player who loses every piece is out; their ally plays on alone.</li>
            <li>
                A player left with only their Raja may <strong>retire</strong> at the start of their turn. Their
                turns are skipped from then on, and taking their Raja scores no one.
            </li>
            <li>If three whole rounds go by with everyone passing, the game ends, and points decide.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }
}
